This is a sample note, to show how a Notebook piece looks. The final text will be written by Michele.

# What happens at a first visit

I listen first. How long, how it started, what makes it worse and what makes it better, what you can no longer do. Then I examine the shoulder: range of motion, strength, the tests that point to which tendon or structure is involved.

Only afterwards do I look at the images. An MRI says a lot but not everything: a cuff tear in a seventy-year-old without pain is not a disease, it is a photograph of age.

# Why the plan sometimes changes

Because the diagnosis on paper and the person in front of me do not always match. A "massive tear" may be well compensated and need nothing. A "small tear" in a forty-year-old bricklayer may deserve repair now, before it grows.

And because there are middle paths that often go untold: waiting with a serious exercise programme, a targeted injection to get out of the acute phase, a smaller operation than the one proposed.

# What you take home

A written plan, with timings. What to do, what to avoid, when to meet again. If surgery is needed, I explain which one, why that one and not another, and what to expect in the months after.

If it is not needed, I say so with the same clarity.
